<%= render "shared/page_header",
      title: "What Elephants Do",
      intro: "Elephants are smart. They live in close families, talk to each other and look after one another. " \
             "The way an animal acts is called its behavior." %>

<div class="section">
  <h2>How elephants get food</h2>
  <ul class="fact-list">
    <li>
      <span class="fact-label">Trunk</span>
      <span class="fact-value">Pulls up grass, picks fruit and leaves, and reaches branches 6 m high. That's about as high as a giraffe!</span>
    </li>
    <li>
      <span class="fact-label">Tusks</span>
      <span class="fact-value">Peel bark off trees and dig up roots.</span>
    </li>
    <li>
      <span class="fact-label">Feet</span>
      <span class="fact-value">Kick up clumps of grass and dig in dry riverbeds to find water.</span>
    </li>
    <li>
      <span class="fact-label">Strength</span>
      <span class="fact-value">Push over whole trees to reach the leaves and fruit at the top.</span>
    </li>
    <li>
      <span class="fact-label">Memory</span>
      <span class="fact-value">The grandma elephant who leads the family remembers where to find food and water in each season, even many years later.</span>
    </li>
  </ul>
</div>

<div class="section">
  <h2>How elephants stay safe</h2>
  <p>
    Grown-up elephants are so big that very few animals try to hunt them. But lions, hyenas and crocodiles in Africa,
    and tigers in Asia, sometimes go after baby elephants. The biggest danger to elephants is people. Some people hunt
    elephants for their tusks, even though it's against the law, and others take over the land where elephants live.
  </p>
  <div class="card-grid">
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Safety in numbers</h3>
      <p>When there's danger, the family makes a circle with the babies safe in the middle.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Warning signs</h3>
      <p>Elephants spread their ears, lift their trunks, trumpet loudly and pretend to charge. This usually scares danger away.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Big and strong</h3>
      <p>If that doesn't work, an elephant can run at about 25 km/h, faster than most people can run, and push back with its tusks and huge body.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Warning calls</h3>
      <p>Elephants make different sounds for different dangers. Scientists think they may even have a special sound that means "humans!"</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="section">
  <h2>Sleeping</h2>
  <div class="stat-row">
    <div class="stat-card">
      <div class="stat-label">In the wild</div>
      <div class="stat-value">About 2 hours</div>
      <p>Of sleep a day, mostly early in the morning.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="stat-card">
      <div class="stat-label">In zoos</div>
      <div class="stat-value">4–6 hours</div>
      <p>Of sleep a night, because there are no wild animals to watch out for.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="info-block">
    <p>
      Wild elephants sleep less than almost any other mammal. They usually sleep standing up! They only lie down for a
      deeper sleep every few days.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="highlight-box">
    <p>
      <strong>Record breakers:</strong> Scientists in Botswana found that some elephant leaders stayed awake for up to
      46 hours in a row. That's almost two whole days!
    </p>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="section">
  <h2>More amazing things elephants do</h2>
  <div class="card-grid">
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Family life</h3>
      <p>An elephant family is made up of mothers, aunts, sisters and babies, led by the grandma. When boys become teenagers, they leave to live alone or with other males.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Talking</h3>
      <p>Elephants talk with low rumbles. Many are too deep for people to hear! The rumbles can travel several kilometres through the air and the ground.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h3>Remembering</h3>
      <p>Elephants have been seen gently touching the bones of elephants that have died and standing quietly beside them, as if they feel sad.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
